  "prediction": "The Atomic Energy Act of 1954 requires a peaceful nuclear cooperation agreement between the US and other countries for significant nuclear transfers. The agreement, known as a Section 123 agreement, sets the terms for nuclear cooperation and must be approved by Congress. The agreement includes nonproliferation criteria, such as guarantees that the transferred nuclear material will not be used for weapons purposes. The AEA also outlines the process for congressional approval, which includes consultation with the House Foreign Affairs and Senate Foreign Relations committees and a 90-day review period. The AEA allows for expedited approval for certain types of agreements, such as those related to research and development. The report also discusses the legislative history of Section 123 agreements and examples of agreements that have been approved outside of the regular legislative process. For instance, the US-Australia nuclear cooperation agreement was approved through legislation in 2014, while the US-India agreement was approved through an executive agreement in 2008. The report also mentions the use of the Hyde Act in 1994 to expedite the approval of the US-France agreement. The report concludes by noting that the AEA provides a framework for peaceful nuclear cooperation while ensuring nonproliferation and that the process for congressional approval is an essential component of this framework.",
  "scored_prediction": "The Atomic Energy Act of 1954 requires a peaceful nuclear cooperation agreement between the US and other countries for significant nuclear transfers. The agreement, known as a Section 123 agreement, sets the terms for nuclear cooperation and must be approved by Congress. The agreement includes nonproliferation criteria, such as guarantees that the transferred nuclear material will not be used for weapons purposes. The AEA also outlines the process for congressional approval, which includes consultation with the House Foreign Affairs and Senate Foreign Relations committees and a 90-day review period. The AEA allows for expedited approval for certain types of agreements, such as those related to research and development. The report also discusses the legislative history of Section 123 agreements and examples of agreements that have been approved outside of the regular legislative process. For instance, the US-Australia nuclear cooperation agreement was approved through legislation in 2014, while the US-India agreement was approved through an executive agreement in 2008. The report also mentions the use of the Hyde Act in 1994 to expedite the approval of the US-France agreement. The report concludes by noting that the AEA provides a framework for peaceful nuclear cooperation while ensuring nonproliferation and that the process for congressional approval is an essential component of this framework.",
  "score": 24.44444397709877,
  "answers": [
    "In order for the United States to engage in significant civilian nuclear cooperation with other states, it must conclude a framework agreement that meets specific requirements under Section 123 of the Atomic Energy Act (AEA). Significant nuclear cooperation includes the export of reactors, critical parts of reactors, and reactor fuel. The AEA also provides for export control licensing procedures and criteria for terminating cooperation. Congressional review is required for Section 123 agreements; the AEA establishes special parliamentary procedures by which Congress may act on a proposed agreement."
